  "question": {
    "text": "In a common emitter configuration with suitable bias, it is given that <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></math> is the load resistance and <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></math> is small-signal dynamic resistance (input side). Then, voltage gain, current gain and power gain are given, respectively, by:&nbsp;<math><mi>&#946;</mi></math> is current gain, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></math> are respectively base, collector, and emitter currents.",
    "image": null
  },
